{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'git show bbd7564:reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/interaction-final.fix.report.md | nl -ba'","aggregated_output":"     1\t<!-- Author: Claude Opus 5.5 (requested effort high), Agent-tool subagent aa0e6fca8e4a6224b. Saved by the coordinator from the hand-back. -->\n     2\t\n     3\t## Needs from other authors (exact)\n     4\t\n     5\t**Controller (`src/core/chart.ts` `measure()`, around line 184).** It still treats any plot scale within 2% of 1 as 1. Under `scale(1.015)` on an ancestor, the layout then believes the plot is 1.5% wider than it is, so the marks overflow and are clipped. Picking stays correct in my layer, because I map against the marks as drawn. The fix is to divide by the computed size: `width = rect.width / k`, with `k = rect.width / parseFloat(getComputedStyle(plot).width)`, and the same for height. Treat `|k − 1| < 1e-5` as 1. That tolerance is only the rounding of the computed value to six significant digits (\"341.531px\" for 341.53125); it is not a transform. Without it, a point drawn exactly on the plot's top edge is picked as outside. That is what emptied T-tooltip-scatter in an intermediate build (`src/dom/mapping.ts` has the same snap).\n     6\t\n     7\t**Controller, finding 12.** I added `readoutMode?: 'floating' | 'strip' | 'none'` to `ControlsState`. `readoutOf()` in `dom/interaction.ts` is the only place it is read: it uses `readoutMode`, else the old `readout` boolean. 'none' means no floating tooltip, no strip and no pin; the datum is still highlighted. The controller's cast can now be dropped.\n     8\t\n     9\t**Public messages (`public.ts`, fixed), C2-8.** Arbitrary drag ranges still have no non-dragging equivalent (WCAG 2.5.7). The smallest answer is two clicks, and that needs a labelled control. The change: add one `Messages` key, for example `selectRange: 'Select range'`, for a toolbar toggle; while it is on, the next two plot clicks set the range's two ends through the same `requestZoom` path. I did not add it because it changes the public interface.\n    10\t\n    11\t**Lookup.** Nothing needed. Overflow rows are built plain in `alignedRows` (focus.ts), as the data agent asked. Bar swatches now use `barPattern` from `scene/bars.ts`.\n    12\t\n    13\t## Results\n    14\t\n    15\tLast lines of each run, on the shared tree built with `npm run build`:\n    16\t\n    17\t| Run | Result |\n    18\t|---|---|\n    19\t| `npm run typecheck` | clean |\n    20\t| `npx vitest run packages/axylo/test/core` | Test Files 13 passed, Tests 207 passed |\n    21\t| chart + a11y, SVG | Test Files 17 passed, Tests 235 passed |\n    22\t| chart + a11y, canvas | Tests 1 failed, 234 passed (235) |\n    23\t| acceptance, SVG | Test Files 13 passed, **Tests 555 passed (555)** |\n    24\t\n    25\t- **The canvas failure** was the P7 memory test in `lifecycle.browser.test.ts`: non-code heap grew 98,556 B against a 65,536 B limit. It passed in the earlier full canvas run and in two reruns of that test alone, and it also passes on the old bundle. I take it as noise under load from the other agents, not a leak.\n    26\t- **An earlier acceptance run had one failure** (A1 compact: `window.Axylo` undefined): another agent rebuilt `dist` (deleted and rewritten) during that run. The file passed 46/46 on its own, and the full rerun above was 555/555.\n    27\t\n    28\t## The state machine (`dom/interaction.ts`, `shown`)\n    29\t\n    30\t- **States:** `none`, `keyboard`, `hover(target | null)`, `held(target, from, rect, anchor, presentation)`, `pinned(ref, target)`.\n    31\t- **A pointer move:**\n    32\t  - pinned: nothing changes.\n    33\t  - hover: becomes held when the pointer rested 250 ms within 2 viewport px on the same datum and that datum's content is on show.\n    34\t  - held: stays while the pointer is inside the hull of the resting point and the content's rectangle (`placement.insideCorridor`). Otherwise it picks afresh on that same move.\n    35\t  - A pointer that has not rested is never held. There are no per-kind holds left; the scatter/donut rectangle hold is gone.\n    36\t- **`commit` reconciles before presenting:**\n    37\t  - A pin keeps its chosen `DatumRef`: rows are rebuilt from that ref's anchor, and the pin ends when the ref is gone, hidden or not drawn, or the presentation is 'none'.\n    38\t  - A hold ends when the datum's viewport anchor, the presentation or the content rectangle changes, and the pointer is picked again at once.\n    39\t  - A hover is picked again from the pointer's viewport position.\n    40\t  - A switch between floating and strip, a rebuild, `clear()`, Escape, leaving the plot's column, scrolling, or opening the table also ends the hold.\n    41\t- **Activation:** a click or tap always picks afresh at the event point. It pins only where the presentation can pin, sets focus, then selects. Empty space selects nothing and ends a pin. Enter uses `focus`.\n    42\t- **Escape:** one window capture listener spends Escape on a drag or any tooltip on show (`preventDefault` plus `stopImmediatePropagation`). Otherwise the proxy takes it for single mode, otherwise it goes to the page.\n    43\t- **Dismissal** is remembered by datum: the x, the slice, or the group's first point; a group that gains coincident points still matches it.\n    44\t- **Focus** is established in `commit` (the first datum when the proxy is focused and focus is null). State text clears the text cache. Whether the text is rewritten is decided by `datumReading` (identity, values and templates), not by subtracting the position sentence.\n    45\t- **Placement** is pure (`dom/placement.ts`). Validity is checked against the visible plot (plot ∩ viewport ∩ each clipping ancestor's padding box less scrollbar, per axis), every control and every keyboard ring, with a shift along the free axis. The hole is tested on all four corners against r0 − 5, and only ring strokes count there. The last resort never covers a control or a ring; if nothing works, nothing floats. The tooltip's maximum size is the visible plot.\n    46\t- **Mapping:** one function (`dom/mapping.ts`), fractional sizes; the limit for rotated, skewed and mirrored ancestors is documented in its comment.\n    47\t\n    48\t## The 16 findings\n    49\t\n    50\t\"Old\" means the test was run against a bundle built from the pre-pass source (HEAD), through `AXYLO_BUNDLE`. Test files are in `packages/axylo/test/browser/chart/` and `packages/axylo/test/core/`.\n    51\t\n    52\t| # | Fixed | Test | Fails on old |\n    53\t|---|---|---|---|\n    54\t| 1 | yes | final-hold, finding 1 (Q3 to Q4, then click gives Q4's exact row; scatter B under A's tooltip; donut hole selects nothing) | yes, all 3 |\n    55\t| 2 | yes | final-pin, finding 2 (active element, value text, ring, tooltip and Enter checked together) | yes |\n    56\t| 3 | yes: the slice list has roving keys while the plot is not ready (one tab stop) | final-pin, finding 3 | yes |\n    57\t| 4 | yes | focus-placement (reviewer's arithmetic table, fallback, property test); final-pin clipped card with hit-tests | Node tests: the old function was DOM-bound, so I could not run it; the reviewer's recorded outputs are the counterexamples. The card test passes on old. |\n    58\t| 5 | yes | final-hold, finding 5 (dense edge line, horizontal bar to strip, isolated scatter point to strip, no-rest converse) | yes, the first 3 |\n    59\t| 6 | yes: pinned content exposed to the AX tree; pinned \"+N more\" is a button that pages through the rows; the strip keeps at least one row | final-pin (AX buttons, ten coincident rows, narrow strip) | AX and paging yes; narrow strip no |\n    60\t| 7 | yes: rows keyed by identity, moved only when out of place | final-pin, press across an append | yes |\n    61\t| 8 | yes | final-hold, finding 8 (stream commit, resize into the strip, with pin and keyboard) | stream commit yes; the other two no |\n    62\t| 9 | yes | final-pin, finding 9 (pin ends when A is hidden; dismissal survives coincident B) | yes, both |\n    63\t| 10 | yes | Node ring test; final-pin single-mode union | yes (browser) |\n    64\t| 11 | yes | focus-placement, hole cases | Node only, as for #4 |\n    65\t| 12 | controller's; my side is `readoutOf` | final-hold, rule 5 (a plot under 140 px never floats) | yes, mostly through the controller's part |\n    66\t| 13 | yes, except rotation and mirroring, which are documented as unsupported | final-pin, `scale(1.015)` and non-uniform scale | 1.015 yes; non-uniform no |\n    67\t| 14 | yes | final-pin, translated `pointOf` with an idle append; focus-reading | yes |\n    68\t| 15 | yes | final-hold, modal dialog with pin and single mode (Escape 3 times); a page listener does not see a spent Escape | yes, both |\n    69\t| 16 | yes | final-hold, `tooltip: false` | yes |\n    70\t\n    71\t## Earlier partials\n    72\t\n    73\t| Finding | Fixed | Test | Fails on old |\n    74\t|---|---|---|---|\n    75\t| C2-7 | yes: overflow rows plain, the total qualified once | focus-reading | yes (2 or 3 \"incomplete\") |\n    76\t| C2-8 | tap now sets the Zoom-in anchor; arbitrary ranges not done (see top) | final-partials | yes |\n    77\t| C2-17 | yes: focus inside the toolbar, table, field, pager or list goes to the figure on unbuild | final-partials (toolbar, jump field) | yes, both |\n    78\t| C2-18 | yes: column's own digits, grouping, decimal and unit; trailing text rejected; displayed dates round-trip; wall time kept | focus-placement (Node grammar); final-partials | yes (browser) |\n    79\t| C2-19 | proxy language follows the value template; table \"No data\" cells and the \" (incomplete)\" suffix tagged; donut missing value tagged | final-partials | yes, all 3 |\n    80\t| C3-6 | yes | final-hold, C3-6 | yes |\n    81\t| C3-7 | yes (`pinchWidth`) | focus-placement; final-partials pinch | yes |\n    82\t| C3-9 | yes, covered by #3, #6 and #7 | as those | as those |\n    83\t\n    84\tOn C2-19's limit: a hidden labelling element would give the name its own language, but axe's label-title-only rule fails a control named only by hidden text, so the name keeps `aria-label`. The name, the role description and the value text therefore share the proxy's single language.\n    85\t\n    86\t## The ten mutations\n    87\t\n    88\tEach was applied to a scratch copy of the source, built into a private bundle and run against the named test.\n    89\t\n    90\t| Mutation | Caught by |\n    91\t|---|---|\n    92\t| 1 | finding-1 test |\n    93\t| 2 | finding-3 test |\n    94\t| 3 | finding-2 test |\n    95\t| 4 | Node rule-6 tests (2 fail) |\n    96\t| 5 | finding-8 stream-commit test |\n    97\t| 6a (pin buttons hidden from AX) | AX buttons test |\n    98\t| 6b (pressed row replaced) | press-across-append test |\n    99\t| 7 (visible text one move behind) | final-hold mutation 7 (strides 1, 7, 23, both directions) |\n   100\t| 8 (touch tap on the second strip button selects the first) | final-pin mutation 8 |\n   101\t| 9 (`change` listener removed) | change-only test |\n   102\t| 10 (area markers of series 7 and 8 swapped in the scene) | final-pin mutation 10 |\n   103\t\n   104\tNot covered: the second half of mutation 4, a strip clipped entirely by an ancestor.\n   105\t\n   106\t## Changes to existing tests (both follow revision 3)\n   107\t\n   108\t- review2 \"strip can be hovered\": a 300 ms rest is now required first.\n   109\t- review2 \"card that clips\": now accepts the 'none' readout for a 97 px plot, and its title was reworded.\n   110\t\n   111\t## Files\n   112\t\n   113\t- New:\n   114\t  - `src/dom/placement.ts`\n   115\t  - `src/dom/mapping.ts`\n   116\t  - `test/core/focus-placement.test.ts`\n   117\t  - `test/core/focus-reading.test.ts`\n   118\t  - `test/browser/chart/final-util.ts`\n   119\t  - `test/browser/chart/final-hold.browser.test.ts`\n   120\t  - `test/browser/chart/final-pin.browser.test.ts`\n   121\t  - `test/browser/chart/final-partials.browser.test.ts`\n   122\t- Rewritten: `src/dom/interaction.ts`, `src/dom/tooltip.ts`.\n   123\t- Edited:\n   124\t  - `src/dom/pointer.ts`\n   125\t  - `src/dom/table.ts` (cell language runs)\n   126\t  - `src/core/focus.ts`\n   127\t  - `src/theme/css.ts` (structure section only: strip height is two of its own line heights; tooltip overflow)\n   128\t  - `test/browser/chart/review2.browser.test.ts`\n   129\t- The scratch directory has been removed. Nothing is committed.\n   130\t\n   131\t## What remains\n   132\t\n   133\t- Two-click range selection (needs the message key above).\n   134\t- Rotated, skewed or mirrored ancestors stay out of scope.\n   135\t- A clipped strip is not tested.\n   136\t- Dates typed with zone abbreviations other than UTC or GMT are accepted only in ISO form.\n   137\t- The pinned tooltip container has no group name (there is no message for one).\n   138\t- The optional \"prepare tooltip and value text before the frame's first write\" was not done.\n   139\t- No screen reader was used. All accessibility claims here are DOM and Chrome AX-tree assertions only.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'git show bbd7564:AGENTS.md | nl -ba'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Working in this repository\n     2\t\n     3\tAxylo is a browser charting library. The product is specified in `docs/02-product-spec.md`; the build plan is `docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md`. Read the sections your task names before writing code.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Rules for every change\n     6\t\n     7\t- **Contracts are fixed.** `packages/axylo/src/core/contracts.ts` and `packages/axylo/src/public.ts` are the interfaces between modules. Do not edit them. If your work needs a change there, stop that part, finish what you can, and describe the exact change you need in your report.\n     8\t- **Stay in your files.** Each task lists the directories it owns. Do not create, edit or delete anything else, including configuration and other modules. Several agents work in this checkout at the same time.\n     9\t- **No runtime dependencies.** Use the platform (`Intl`, typed arrays). Dev dependencies already installed: TypeScript, Vitest, fast-check, esbuild, Playwright (system Chrome), axe-core. The network is not available; do not try to install anything.\n    10\t- **No DOM at import time.** A module that needs the DOM takes it as a parameter or touches it inside a function. Pure stages (data, scales, formatting, layout, scene) must run in Node.\n    11\t- **Hot paths use typed arrays.** No object per data point, no array of arrays per data point, no copying of large columns that the contracts say are borrowed.\n    12\t- **Never guess silently.** Anything that cannot be drawn as given is reported through the `DiagnosticSink` with the code from spec section 3.5.\n    13\t- **Tests come from the spec.** Expected values are derived from the specification and the fixture data, never by running the implementation and pasting its output. Use fast-check property tests for invariants (\"for any data …\"). A test you cannot make pass is reported as failing; do not weaken it or change its expected value.\n    14\t- **TypeScript strict, no `any`** in exported signatures. `__DEV__` is a build-time boolean (true in tests).\n    15\t- **Comments say why**, not what. Names are plain words.\n    16\t- **Do not commit.** The coordinator commits.\n    17\t\n    18\t## Commands\n    19\t\n    20\t```bash\n    21\tnpm run typecheck      # tsc over the library, its tests, and the API examples in docs/api\n    22\tnpm test               # unit and property tests (Node)\n    23\tnpm run build          # packages/axylo/dist\n    24\tnpm run test:browser   # browser tests against the built bundle, in system Chrome\n    25\t```\n    26\t\n    27\t## Your report\n    28\t\n    29\tEnd with a short report: what you built (files), what you verified and how (paste the last lines of the test run), what is not done or not verified, any contract change you need, and any place where the spec was ambiguous and what you assumed. Keep \"ran and saw\" apart from \"expect\".\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'git show bbd7564:docs/decisions/0004-tooltip-and-focused-text.md | nl -ba'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'git show bbd7564:docs/decisions/0004-tooltip-and-focused-text.md | nl -ba'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Decision 0004: where the tooltip goes, and when the focused point's text changes\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: decided by the coordinator on 2026-11-18 after the second review of the interaction layer (`reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core3.interaction.review.md`, new findings 3 and 4). Not rehearsed with a screen reader or a screen magnifier; both parts need that before they are called verified.\n     4\t\n     5\t## 1. The tooltip is anchored to the datum and never takes the pointer (revision 3)\n     6\t\n     7\tRevision 1 (placed across the reading axis, offset from the pointer, interactive while hovered) was implemented three times and still failed the acceptance tests: because the tooltip's position followed the pointer it moved away when approached, because it took pointer events it stalled the readout or swallowed the click whenever a move landed on it, and it could cover controls and the focus ring. Revision 2 removes the causes instead of tuning distances. Revision 3 (after the final review, `reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core-final.interaction.review.md`) replaces revision 2's separate hold rules with one, and adds rules 8 to 10 and the limits in rules 5 and 6.\n     8\t\n     9\t**Problem.** WCAG 1.4.13 requires content shown on hover to stay when the pointer moves onto it. Spec I1 requires the readout to name the datum at the pointer on every move, the tooltip not to cover the active mark, to stay inside the viewport, and to fall back to a fixed readout when it cannot fit. On a line chart the whole plot picks, so anything that takes pointer events inside the plot blocks picking.\n    10\t\n    11\t**Rules.**\n    12\t\n    13\t1. **Position depends on the datum only.** For the datum on show, the tooltip is placed against the bounding box of that datum's active marks (all series at that x for line, area and bar; the point for scatter; the slice for donut), enlarged by the focus ring's width. The pointer's own position never enters the computation, so the tooltip does not move while the datum is unchanged, and it cannot run away from an approaching pointer.\n    14\t2. **Candidates, in order:** above the box, below it, then to the right and to the left of it (for horizontal bars: right, left, above, below). Along the other axis it is centred on the box and shifted only as far as needed. A candidate is valid when the tooltip lies wholly inside the plot rectangle, inside the viewport and inside every clipping ancestor, and overlaps neither the box nor any control. The first valid candidate is used. For a donut the first candidate is the hole, when the tooltip fits inside it.\n    15\t3. **A hovered tooltip never takes pointer events.** It is `pointer-events: none`. The plot keeps receiving every move, so picking never stalls at any speed, and a click goes to the plot and selects the datum under the pointer. Moving the pointer onto the tooltip therefore cannot hide it: on line, area and bar the tooltip stays for as long as the pointer's position along the reading axis still picks the same datum, which a straight approach across the reading axis does; on scatter and donut, while the pointer is inside the tooltip's rectangle the datum is held (computed from coordinates in the plot's own move handler, not by letting the tooltip take the event).\n    16\t3b. **A rest holds the datum along the way to the content (revision 3).** Rule 3 alone lets the content change whenever the straight path to it crosses positions that pick something else, and the final review found such paths for every placement: a tooltip beside a tall bar, a donut's tooltip in the hole, a scatter point's tooltip, a tooltip above a point in a dense line near the plot's edge, and the strip under a small plot. So one rule covers all of them, replacing the per-placement cases of revision 2 (including the unconditional hold inside a scatter or donut tooltip's rectangle, which is withdrawn): once the pointer has rested on a datum for 250 ms (no move larger than 2 CSS px, measured in viewport pixels), that datum is held while the pointer stays inside the corridor between its resting position and the rectangle of the content shown for it (the floating tooltip, or the strip in readout mode), that is, the convex hull of the point and the rectangle, and inside the rectangle itself. Leaving the corridor resumes picking on that same move. A pointer that has not rested is never held, in any placement, so a sweep never stalls.\n    17\t   - **The hold affects what is shown, never what is activated.** A click or tap always picks afresh from the pointer's position; if that is a different datum from the one held, the click selects and pins the datum under the pointer. A click on empty space (a donut's hole, outside a scatter point's radius) selects nothing.\n    18\t   - **The hold belongs to one datum, one geometry and one presentation.** It ends when the displayed frame changes that datum's anchor or the content's rectangle, when the presentation switches between floating and strip, on a pin, on Escape, when the pointer leaves the plot and the content, and on any rebuild. After it ends, picking is from the pointer's position at once, without waiting for a move.\n    19\t4. **Rows are not controls while hovered.** A hovered tooltip is text. Clicking or tapping the plot pins the tooltip for that datum; a pinned tooltip takes pointer events, stays until Escape, a click elsewhere, or its datum leaves the displayed frame, and its rows are buttons of at least 24 × 24 CSS px (WCAG 2.5.8) that select a series. Keyboard focus shows the same tooltip by the same placement; it is never over the focus ring because the box includes the ring.\n    20\t5. **Readout mode is decided by layout, not by hover.** When the plot, laid out without a strip, would be narrower than 240 CSS px or shorter than 140 CSS px, the figure is in readout mode: a strip of two text lines (two actual line heights of the strip's own font, whatever the page's text size) is reserved under the plot in the layout, and the content that would be in the tooltip is written there as running text (\"x: series value; series value; +N more\"), wrapping to the two lines and ending in \"+N more\" when it does not fit; at least the x and one row are always shown. The strip is part of the figure's flow: it covers nothing and nothing covers it. In readout mode there is no floating tooltip. A pinned datum makes the strip's entries buttons of 24 px height. If reserving the strip would leave the plot under the minimum of spec L8 (80 px high), no strip is reserved and pointing shows no readout at all: the datum is still highlighted, and its values are in the keyboard reading and the table. Such a plot is never put into floating mode.\n    21\t6. **When no candidate is valid outside readout mode** (a tooltip taller than the room around a tall stack, for example), rows are dropped from the end and counted in \"+N more\" until a candidate is valid. The tooltip is never wider or taller than the visible part of the plot (its content wraps; the visible part is the plot rectangle cut by the viewport and by every clipping ancestor's real clipping edge). If no candidate is valid with one row, the candidate that overlaps the active box least is used, shifted along its free axis as far as needed to avoid every control and every focus ring: controls and focus rings are never covered, in any case. This is the only case in which the active mark may be partly covered, and the table still has every value.\n    22\t7. Escape dismisses a hovered or pinned tooltip without moving the pointer, until the pointer reaches a different datum. One Escape does one thing: while a tooltip, a pin or a drag is active, the chart consumes the key and nothing else reacts to it (a surrounding dialog stays open, single-series reading stays on); the next Escape goes to whatever is next.\n    23\t8. **A pin is one datum.** The pin remembers the datum the reader chose. Other data that come to share its position are listed with it while it is displayed; when it leaves the displayed frame or is hidden, the pin ends, whatever else is at that position. A dismissal is remembered for that datum in the same way. With `tooltip: false` nothing can be pinned: a click still selects and takes focus.\n    24\t9. **Pinned content is real.** A pinned tooltip or strip is exposed to assistive technology with its row buttons named by their text; rows keep their element across redraws while their identity is unchanged, so a press that spans a data update still completes.\n    25\t10. **Coordinates.** Picking, anchors, clipping and placement use one mapping between the plot's layout pixels and viewport pixels, taken from fractional sizes, so a translated or scaled ancestor (any positive scale, uniform or not) is exact. Rotated, skewed or mirrored ancestors are not supported in 1.0; the limits section of the spec says so.\n    26\t\n    27\tRejected: revision 1; an interactive hovered tooltip with a \"safe triangle\" (still ambiguous when the reading axis and the approach coincide); always using the readout (loses the association between the values and the mark on plots that have room).\n    28\t\n    29\tWhat \"can itself be hovered\" (spec I1) means under this decision, for the tests: the pointer can be moved straight from the datum onto the tooltip's rectangle, and the tooltip is still shown, for the same datum, when the pointer is inside it. It does not mean the tooltip is the hit-test target.\n    30\t\n    31\t## 2. The focused point's text changes only for a reason the reader would name\n    32\t\n    33\t**Problem.** The plot's keyboard proxy is a native range whose value text describes the focused point, ending with its position (\"Point 4 of 120\"). While data streams in, the count changes on every append, so the text of a focused control changes without the reader acting. Spec A7 forbids unrequested announcements; whether a given screen reader speaks such a change cannot be tested here.\n    34\t\n    35\t**Rule.** The range's bounds and numeric value are always kept true to the displayed frame, silently. Its value text is rewritten only when:\n    36\t\n    37\t1. the reader moves (a key, an assistive adjustment, a pointer or touch selection that takes focus),\n    38\t2. the focused datum's own values change (the same x and series now read differently),\n    39\t3. the focused datum leaves the frame (the text must then say where focus went), or\n    40\t4. the plot regains focus.\n    41\t\n    42\tAn append that only changes the count or the ordinal of an unchanged datum does not rewrite the text. The position in the text can therefore be out of date until the next of the four events; it is recomputed then.\n    43\t\n    44\tTo check with a screen reader when one is available (added to decision 0001's list): idle appends with the plot focused are silent; an eviction of the focused point is spoken once; an increment immediately after an append lands on the expected neighbour and reads the current count.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
