{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"git show bbd7564:reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core-final.interaction.review.md | sed -n '9,111p' | sed -E 's/\\\\[[\"'^]]*'\"\\\\]\\\\([\"'^)]*'\"\\\\)//g'\"","aggregated_output":"| # | Severity and evidence | Reproduction, consequence, requirement and fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **B · Trace** | **A mouse click can select the held hover datum.** Rest on a full-height Q3 bar for 250 ms. Move along its corridor into Q4, then click while Q3 is still shown. `pointerup` calls `pointAt`, which returns the corridor’s Q3. The subsequent `primary` and `select` operate on Q3. On scatter, the same thing happens when B lies under A’s tooltip rectangle. Touch already does a fresh pick. Use a fresh `lookup.pick` at mouse release for activation, independently of the hover hold. A click in an empty part of a donut hole should yield no selection. **I1, I4, rules 3 and 4.**  ·  |\n| 2 | **B · Node + trace** | **Reset can restore the chart without restoring its keyboard datum.** Focus the first point of a line with x values `0,1,99,100`, zoom to `[25,75]`, then press `0`. In Node, the empty lookup resolved the old focus to `null`, and the reset lookup had a valid first point at x=0. In the DOM path, `focus` stays null. The empty branch wrote the state message but retained `shownText`; on Reset the old cached first-point reading can suppress the write that should replace “No data.” `current()` also has no keyboard target to highlight. Establish and store one effective focus before updating the range, ring and tooltip. Invalidate the text cache whenever state text replaces datum text. **A2, A3, L7, decision 0004 part 2, WCAG 4.1.2.**  ·  |\n| 3 | **B · Node + trace** | **All-zero donut slices are mouse-only actions.** Create two zero slices, leave the legend enabled and set `table:false`. Node gave `ready=false`, two addressable slices and no table. Each list button calls `select`, each has `tabIndex=-1`, the proxy is hidden, and no toolbar item takes the reader through the list. Tab skips both actions. Give the slice list roving keyboard access in this state, or keep a usable slice-navigation proxy while showing the empty message. **F8, A2, I4, WCAG 2.1.1.**  ·  |\n| 4 | **B · Node + trace** | **The last placement can cover a control or escape its clipping region.** With a 320 px tip and usable x range `[4,296]`, production placement returned x=4, so the right edge was 324. With a full-height active box and controls across the two side candidates, it returned `(138,80)`, directly over a control, despite free space farther up that side. The fallback ranks only overlap with the datum. Ordinary candidates center against the plot before testing the clip, and clipping uses ancestor border boxes on both axes. Constrain the measured content to the actual visible region, shift along the permitted axis, preserve control and focus exclusions in fallback, and define a reachable presentation when one row cannot fit. Use the ancestor’s actual clipping edge. **I1, A2, A6, rules 2 and 6.**  ·  |\n| 5 | **B · Node geometry + trace** | **Some shown content has no hoverable route, even after resting.** Put line points at the left plot edge and 2 px to its right. The first point’s picking interval ends about 1 px from the edge. An above/below tooltip starts at least 4 px in. Every point inside it picks a different x, and `acrossReadingAxis` disables the corridor. In readout mode, move down from an upper horizontal-bar category to the strip. The pointer crosses other categories first. From an isolated scatter point it can cross more than 24 px of empty plot and lose the readout altogether. The special strip hold starts only *below* the marked plot. Base the hold on whether the actual route preserves the pick, including routes to the strip. Make that route available whenever the additional content is shown. **I1, rules 3/3b/5, WCAG 1.4.13.**  ·  ·  |\n| 6 | **M · Trace** | **Pinned choices are hidden from accessibility APIs, and truncation can remove the only pointer action for a row.** Pin two series. The visible buttons remain descendants of `aria-hidden=\"true\"` in both presentations. Pin ten coincident scatter rows of one series and the omitted rows have no individual pointer target. The table exposes values, but its cells do not perform `select`. A narrow strip can drop even its last row. Expose actionable content on pin, retain the two-stop keyboard model through the existing composite navigation, and provide an operable way to choose overflow rows. **I4, A2, A3, rule 4, WCAG 4.1.2.**  ·  ·  |\n| 7 | **M · Trace** | **A stream commit can discard a row button during a press.** Pin a tooltip, press its secondary row, append an unrelated point and let the frame commit before release. Every commit marks presentation dirty. Both renderers of tooltip content replace the button nodes, and the floating path aborts their listeners. The original pressed button cannot receive its normal click. The same replacement can disrupt a touch press in the strip. Keep the button for a stable row identity through a redraw and update its text and position in place. **I4, A7, rule 4.**  ·  ·  |\n| 8 | **M · Trace** | **A corridor can outlive its datum, geometry or presentation.** Rest on Q3 and enter its corridor above Q4. Let a stream or resize commit with the pointer still. Commit re-picks Q4 without consulting the corridor. The next move inside the old hull can restore Q3. Switching to the strip hides the floating element but retains `heldTip`, `rest` and `corridor`. The existing-hull and scatter/donut rectangle checks can therefore use an invisible floating tooltip. Bind the hold to its datum and current geometry, reconcile it during commit, and clear or rebuild it on presentation change, dismissal and rebuild. Measure the 2 px rest in viewport CSS pixels. **I1, rules 3, 3b, 5 and 7.**  ·  ·  |\n| 9 | **M · Node + trace** | **A scatter pin or dismissal follows the coincident group instead of the datum the reader chose.** Pin A alone at `(5,5)`, append coincident B, then hide A using the keyboard legend. Node returned `[B,A]` after append. After hiding A, its ordinal was `-1` and B was still pickable. `stillPinned` accepts B and keeps the pin. Conversely, dismiss A, then append B under the stationary pointer. The changed count and ref list make it a different dismissal target and can reopen the tooltip. Store the originally chosen `DatumRef` separately from the rows shown. Rebuild those rows while that ref is displayed and end the pin when it leaves. Use the same identity distinction for Escape. **I1, A7, rules 4 and 7, `DatumRef`/`PickTarget`.**  ·  |\n| 10 | **M · Node + trace** | **Placement can exclude the wrong focus geometry.** Focus A with the keyboard, then move the mouse over nearby B without blurring the proxy. The overlay still draws A’s visible ring, but placement protects only B. A normal, first-choice candidate can cover A’s entire datum ring. In narrowed keyboard mode the tooltip still lists every series at x, but only the narrowed ref contributes to its protected box. On a two-series area I measured a 229.6 px union in all mode and a zero-height box at the same x in single mode. Protect the visible keyboard ring independently and use every displayed series at x for the decision’s placement box. **A2, I1, rules 1 and 4.**  ·  ·  |\n| 11 | **M · Node** | **“Hole first” tests the center, not whether the tooltip fits in the hole.** A 160×30 tip was accepted at the center of a 96 px diameter hole when the selected slice’s box was clear. Conversely, a 40×20 tip that fit the hole was placed outside it when the selected slice’s bounding rectangle covered the center. A real 99% slice produced exactly that enclosing box. Test the four tooltip corners against the inner radius with clearance for the inner outline, and test the actual focus-ring strokes. Treat the hole as an explicit exception to the filled bounding rectangle. **I1, rules 1 and 2.**  ·  ·  |\n| 12 | **M · Node + trace** | **Short readable plots can bypass readout mode.** The recording host produced a **496×96** plot with `readout=false`. The extra test requires 80 px to remain after subtracting the strip. At the default 48 px strip height, that excludes otherwise readable plots below 128 px high. Large text introduces another mismatch. With a 40 px parent font, the strip font is 35 px, its two line boxes need 98 px, and its height expression gives 85.75 px. Define how I1 and L8 share the available height, implement that rule explicitly, and size the strip from two actual line heights. **I1, L8, A6, rule 5.**  ·  |\n| 13 | **M · Node + trace** | **Transformed ancestors can still give the wrong pick and placement.** Positive `scale(2)` returned the correct factors. For a 1,000 px element scaled to 1,015 px, `cssScale` returned 1. A 90° rotation or `scaleX(-1)` also cannot be inverted by dividing two positive bounding-box dimensions. A visually rightmost click on the reflected plot is interpreted at the opposite end. Use one reversible local-to-viewport transform for measurement, picking, clipping and placement. **I1, A2, rule 2.**  ·  ·  |\n| 14 | **M · Node + trace** | **A translated range reading can change on an idle append.** Use `pointOf: \"{series} {x} {value} [{index}/{count}]\"`. Keep the first scatter point focused and append one point. The strings used by the change detector were `y 0 2 [1/2]` and `y 0 2 [1/3]`. It tries to subtract the independently translated `position` sentence from `pointOf`; the sentence need never occur there. Compare the datum’s semantic reading and identity before formatting its position. **A7, A10, decision 0004 part 2.**  ·  |\n| 15 | **M · Trace** | **One Escape can close two things.** Mouse-pin a point in a dialog, leaving the proxy focused, then press Escape. Document capture dismisses the pin and lets the same event continue. A dialog can close too. If the proxy was in single-series mode, its handler can also leave that mode on this first Escape. Spend one Escape on the active chart operation, then let a subsequent unhandled Escape reach the page. **Decision 0001, A2, rule 7.**  ·  |\n| 16 | **M · Trace** | **Disabling tooltips still creates an invisible pin.** Set `tooltip:false`, click A and move over B. The click sets `pinned=true`, later moves return before picking, and Escape requires `tooltip.visible` to release the pin. The overlay and public hover can stay at A. Keep click selection and focus takeover, and enter pinned state only for a presentation that can actually be pinned. **I1, rule 4, `Overlay`.**  ·  ·  |\n\n## Disposition of the 33 earlier findings\n\n`C2` denotes the first review. `C3` denotes the eleven new findings in part 2 of the second review. The severity here is the **original** severity. Numbered references refer to the release findings above.\n\n| Prior finding | Disposition | Sequence checked, evidence and remaining action |\n|---|---|---|\n| C2-1 · B · Paused call order | **Fixed** | **Trace.** Pause → append A → replace with B → append C → Resume. Accepted replacement clears the old held frame. Rejected replacement preserves it, and paused append validates before publishing. I6.  |\n| C2-2 · B · Category identity | **Fixed for the reported slide** | **Trace.** On `A,B,C → B,C,D`, focused B follows B, focused A moves to B, and a pin or dismissal on A ends. A transition with no surviving focus still needs the recovery in #2. A3, A7.  |\n| C2-3 · B · Empty-window recovery | **Fixed with a new defect** | **Node + trace.** Empty zoom retains `0`, Zoom out and Reset. Loading is distinguished from empty, Resume is available, and all-zero slices can be listed with the table disabled. Reset can leave stale focus, and the new slice actions lack keyboard access. #2–3. I3, I6, L7.  |\n| C2-4 · B · Frozen native range | **Fixed** | **Trace.** Focus a one-point chart and append. The native maximum and ordinal are synchronized independently of the text guard. The remaining state-text and translation defects are #2 and #14. A3, A7.  |\n| C2-5 · M · Retained revisions and caller rows | **Fixed for the reported path** | **Trace.** With displayed A, accepted B and a later paused append, retirement accounts separately for the on-screen and pending frame. It freezes the on-screen row source before advancing its retirement floor. I did not measure memory. I6, plan §1.5.  |\n| C2-6 · M · Native range intent | **Fixed** | **Trace.** Home at the first point leaves no pending direction. `input` and `change` resolve the absolute ordinal, duplicate notifications are guarded, and focus takeover reads the pointer datum first. A2, A3.  |\n| C2-7 · M · Keyboard information loss | **Partly fixed** | **Node + trace.** Hidden-only x positions, ordinary totals, narrowed scatter steps and the full coincidence count are accounted for. With ten stacked series and one gap, I still got **three** `incomplete` qualifications, on S8, S9 and the total. Format the overflow series from unqualified row values and qualify the total once. I1, A3, §3.6.  ·  |\n| C2-8 · M · Single-pointer pan and selection | **Partly fixed** | **Node + trace.** Tap selects, and pan buttons exist. From `[25,75]` in `[0,100]`, keyboard pan gave `[30,80]`, button pan `[50,100]`. Tap never assigns the `focus` used by Zoom in. Arbitrary drag endpoints and overflow row selections still lack equivalent controls. Retain the touch anchor and offer start/end clicks or fields for exact range selection. I3, I4, WCAG 2.5.7.  ·  |\n| C2-9 · M · Unattainable zoom minimum | **Fixed for the reported cases** | **Node.** Two or three positions over `[0,100]` halve, and `[10¹⁶,10¹⁶+4]` narrows to a representable width of 2. Under the amended matrix, category line, area and bar all exclude zoom. I3, §3.6.  |\n| C2-10 · M · Gesture ownership and cancellation | **Fixed** | **Trace.** Escape releases a captured drag. Pinch retains its original two IDs, cancellation drops queued work, ordinary lift keeps the final queued move, and the remaining finger cannot become a tap. The horizontal midpoint uses the upward scale direction. I3, I4.  ·  |\n| C2-11 · M · Stale scatter pin | **Partly fixed** | **Node + trace.** Pin `(5,100)` and restrict y to `[0,10]`: the missing anchor ends the pin. Newly coincident rows are re-picked. The originally pinned ref can still be replaced by another group member. Fix #9. I1, A7.  |\n| C2-12 · M · Clipped or transformed tooltip | **Partly fixed** | **Node + trace.** Simple positive scaling and pinned/keyboard scroll repositioning are present. Overwide fallback, ancestor clipping, and nontrivial transforms remain. Fix #4 and #13. I1, A2, A6.  |\n| C2-13 · M · Narrow readout | **Partly fixed** | **Trace.** The strip occupies its own flow space, and a displayed pinned row can receive a click. Approaching it can still change or lose the datum. Fix #5 and #12. I1, I4, WCAG 1.4.13.  |\n| C2-14 · M · Invisible arcless focus | **Fixed for the reported mixed donut** | **Trace.** With zero followed by a positive slice, the plot outline is inset and the current zero slice has a visible list outline. The all-zero action case is #3. F8, A2.  ·  |\n| C2-15 · M · Open-table layout | **Fixed for the reported cases** | **Trace.** Open a table, move the legend right and shrink the card. The table is positioned in the responsive plot column. Its scroll region flexes and the disclosure stays outside the overlay. A4, A6.  ·  |\n| C2-16 · M · Unrequested page-count announcement | **Fixed at the DOM level** | **Trace.** Append row 201 with page one open. The status switches to `aria-live=\"off\"` before changing the count. Reader paging enables polite updates. A7.  |\n| C2-17 · M · Focus lost on removal or reorder | **Partly fixed** | **Trace.** Reordering a surviving button restores it, and collapsing the pager transfers focus to the scroll region. Change to compact with the toolbar or table field focused: `handOff` handles only the proxy, and teardown can then remove the destination control. Hand off focus from any disappearing chart-owned subtree. A2, A4, WCAG 2.4.3.  ·  |\n| C2-18 · M · Jump-to input | **Partly fixed** | **Trace.** Exact ordinary categories, common grouped numbers and associated visible errors improved. `12garbage` is accepted as 12, the number grammar assumes ASCII digits and three-digit groups, and the displayed wall-time fallback returns only the date. Parse the actual field format, locale and unit, preserve wall time, and reject extraneous text. A4, WCAG 3.3.1.  ·  |\n| C2-19 · M · Language and replaceable text | **Partly fixed** | **Trace.** Plot instructions, point templates, note runs and default summary language exist. On a German page, translating just `plotLabel` removes English marking from the remaining English role/value. Table cells and donut missing-value text also lack per-string marking. Put names and descriptions on appropriately tagged text elements and carry language to generated cells and runs. A3, A10, WCAG 3.1.2.  ·  |\n| C2-20 · M · Stale axis unit | **Fixed** | **Node.** Changing only the second series from `ms` to `s` changes the effective shared title and layout key. L6.  |\n| C2-21 · M · Accessibility evidence | **Partly fixed** | **Trace.** The acceptance suite checks real focus, role, painted ring geometry and more state preconditions. The point text still comes from the DOM, pinned actions lack a positive AX assertion, the five rehearsals are absent, and the published A9 page is a placeholder. Publish the criterion-to-test/author-obligation table with its test-existence check and identify the manual evidence gap. A2, A3, A7–A9.  ·  |\n| C2-22 · m · Contrast dash in swatches | **Fixed** | **Trace.** Switch to increased contrast or forced colors. The line swatch carries both dash patterns and CSS selects the contrast pattern. A5, F9.  |\n| C3-1 · B · Narrowed scatter hangs | **Fixed** | **Trace.** For `A0,B1,A2,B3,A4`, an absolute adjustment to ordinal 2 uses bounded global steps and leaves narrowed mode when it reaches A2. There is a synthetic regression for exactly this input. A2, A3.  ·  |\n| C3-2 · B · Table covers right toolbar | **Fixed** | **Trace.** Open View as table with `legend:'right'`. Focus enters the scroll region and the disclosure remains in the adjacent column. A2, A4, WCAG 2.4.11.  |\n| C3-3 · M · Pointer-speed-dependent tooltip | **Fixed with a new defect** | **Trace.** The old tooltip no longer follows the pointer or intercepts a hovered click. Coordinate-based holding can nevertheless select the old datum, and a required hover route can fail. Fix #1, #5 and #8. I1, I4.  |\n| C3-4 · M · Focused streaming text churn | **Partly fixed** | **Node + trace.** Default position-only changes can stay silent at the attribute level. Translated position syntax defeats the comparison. Fix #14. A7, A10.  |\n| C3-5 · M · Replacement splits text and focus | **Fixed for epoch replacement** | **Trace.** Replace the data while keyboard-focused. The epoch branch clears old presentation identity and stores the new effective focus before writing. Same-epoch recovery still needs #2. A2, A3.  |\n| C3-6 · M · Compact or too-small transition | **Partly fixed** | **Trace.** A focused proxy hands off, and compact preparation clears the old overlay before dropping the frame. Other focused subtrees can be removed, and `clear()` leaves the corridor, held rectangle and rest state. Apply C2-17 and #8 on every rebuild. A2, C1.  ·  |\n| C3-7 · M · Zoom-in widens | **Fixed for the reported key/wheel path** | **Node + trace.** `+` on public window `[40,45]` with minimum 20 produces no widening. Pinch still has its own arithmetic: spreading the fingers from that window can impose width 20. Apply the same directional guard to pinch. I3, I4.  ·  |\n| C3-8 · M · Misleading unavailable slider | **Fixed with a new defect** | **Trace.** Loading cannot masquerade as an empty zoom. Hiding every series sets a one-position range and leaves legend recovery. Recovery of the actual datum/text cache is incomplete as in #2. L7, A2, A3.  |\n| C3-9 · M · Unusable touch targets | **Partly fixed** | **Trace.** Displayed pin rows and slice-list choices have 24 px minima, and opening the table closes a pin. Truncated rows lack selection, pinned buttons are AX-hidden, and an all-zero list lacks keyboard access. Fix #3, #6 and #7. I4, A2, F8.  |\n| C3-10 · M · Gesture work crosses owners | **Fixed for the reported sequences** | **Trace.** Blur, detach, cancellation and teardown cancel pending pinch work. A third finger cannot substitute into the active pair, and a nonzoomable mouse press is captured through release. I3, I4.  ·  |\n| C3-11 · M · Bar cue repeats at 33 | **Fixed by specification amendment** | **Trace.** Series 9 and 33 still repeat. Current F9 explicitly permits repetition beyond 32, and acceptance checks all 32 combinations. No implementation change is required for this finding.  ·  |\n\n## Decision 0004 revision 2\n\nLine and area use the aligned overlay. All four bar combinations use the same aligned path with their own bar rectangles, and horizontal orientation changes candidate order. Scatter supplies the point overlay and series-local stepping. Donut supplies slice geometry and its slice list. I found no missing per-kind renderer or interaction handler in the compositions. The failures below occur after the correct kind has been selected.  ·  ·  ·  · \n\n| Rule | Verdict across kinds and input states |\n|---|---|\n| **1 · Datum anchor** | **Partial.** Floating position is independent of pointer coordinates for hover, pin and keyboard. Grouped bars use the union of lanes, stacked bars use segment extents, and line/area use point anchors. Narrowed keyboard mode and a separate keyboard ring are exceptions (#10). A target with no anchor falls back to the plot center, including a line x containing only gaps. The strip has no floating placement. |\n| **2 · Candidate order and validity** | **Partial.** Vertical bar, line, area and scatter try above/below/right/left. Horizontal grouped and stacked bars try right/left/above/below. Donut prepends the center. Ordering works on an unobstructed plot. Hole containment, clipped alignment, transforms and fallback exclusions fail (#4, #11, #13). Pinning can legitimately change the chosen side because its rows are taller. |\n| **3 · Pointer-transparent hover** | **Partial.** Floating hover and keyboard-shown text are pointer-transparent. Pin rows take events. A plotted move reaches the handler, but the handler can override the physical pick, including during a click. Scatter/donut unconditionally hold a stored tooltip rectangle, even without rest and sometimes after the rectangle has disappeared (#1, #8). |\n| **3b · Rest and corridor** | **Partial.** On a stable floating placement, the first move after 250 ms can open the convex hull even when it moves only 1 px across a band edge. Exiting the hull resumes picking on that move. Leaving the plot normally clears rest, hull and held rectangle, so re-entering starts fresh. Commits and presentation switches do not give the same guarantee. The timer also belongs to a position without recording which datum occupied it throughout the rest. Across-axis and strip routes can fail (#5, #8). |\n| **4 · Pin and row actions** | **Partial.** Normal touch taps pick afresh, normal pins survive pointer leave, and displayed rows become buttons. Aligned category pins follow the exact category, and ordinary numeric pins disappear when their x leaves the view. A surviving datum is positioned from its current anchor. Scatter group identity, mouse activation, accessible row actions and presses spanning commits fail (#1, #6, #7, #9). Keyboard movement also unpins, including an endpoint key that leaves the datum unchanged. The decision should specify keyboard takeover explicitly. |\n| **5 · Reserved readout** | **Partial.** When enabled, the strip is in flow under the plot, remains reserved while hidden, uses the same model and counts omitted rows. The implementation can select floating mode below the specified threshold, clip large text, or preserve an obsolete floating hold (#5, #8, #12). An ordinary focused or pinned datum is rendered in the new presentation after resize. An unpinned hover is re-picked using its saved plot-local coordinates, which can be stale if the plot origin moved. |\n| **6 · Row dropping and fallback** | **Partial.** Floating rows drop from the end through one row, with `+N more` recomputed from the full count. The final ranking minimizes overlap among the clamped candidates it generated. It can cover a control, cover the entire focused mark or exceed the visible rectangle (#4, #10). Rule 6 only grants an exception for partial mark coverage. |\n| **7 · Escape** | **Partial.** Ordinary hover and pin close in both presentations. A stationary pointer at an unchanged ordinary target keeps them closed. Keyboard-owned Escape hides the tip, the next Escape leaves single mode, and an idle Escape passes through. A captured drag consumes Escape. Pointer-owned Escape can also reach the page, while group churn and stale holds can reopen or prolong dismissal (#8, #9, #15). |\n\n**Placement arithmetic I ran.** These are outputs from the production tooltip function with supplied element dimensions. They establish candidate selection independently of browser layout or painting.\n\n| Input in CSS pixels | Returned top-left or consequence |\n|---|---|\n| 300×200 plot, 4 px inset, 100×40 tip, point datum at top-left / top-right / bottom-left / bottom-right | `(4,18)` / `(196,18)` / `(4,142)` / `(196,142)`. Every floating rectangle fit. The first rectangle is also the dense-edge hover counterexample in #5. |\n| Full-height box, then full-width horizontal-bar box | Right at `(138,80)` for the first. Above at `(100,27)` for the horizontal bar after right and left failed. |\n| Both side candidates covered by controls | `(138,80)`, covering the right control. A shift up on that same side was available. |\n| Ancestor visible only from x=80 through 220 | `(98,90)` to the right. The preferred above candidate could have fit at x=80, but its along-axis position was clamped only to the plot. |\n| 320 px wide tip in 292 px of usable plot | `(4,32)`, extending 28 px beyond the usable right edge. |\n| Donut hole, too-wide tip / small tip and enclosing slice box | Oversized tip accepted at the center / small fitting tip sent to the right. |\n\n**Two-tone ring.** At the upper-left point, Node produced a datum-ring centerline from `(2,2)` to `(8,8)` with a 4 px surround and 2 px ink. The surround reached the plot edge and the ink remained inside. The CSS plot outline is painted above the datum overlay. Where the two coincide, the plot outline can replace the coincident segment with the same two tones, while the datum’s inward legs remain visible. That crossing alone does not establish lost focus. A tooltip covering the independent datum ring is the actionable A2 defect in #10. I did not verify rasterization at these intersections.  · \n\nThe palette reviewer measured **5.89:1 light and 14.43:1 dark** between focus ink and its opaque surround. The surround falling below 3:1 against all sixteen 25% fills and six of sixteen 80% fills **does not by itself fail the indicator under 1.4.11**. The visible focus ink has its own adjacent background. Assess separately whether the fill is required to understand the graphic and whether its edge, marker or separator supplies the necessary contrast. L2 also makes a product promise about marks. Amend decision 0003’s claim that the surround reaches 3:1 against every touched mark. Document the measured ink-to-surround ratios, the opaque-mark measurements, the translucent-fill exceptions and which visible features carry the information.  ·  · \n\n**Decision versus WCAG.**\n\n- **1.4.13 AA.** Pointer transparency is compatible with hoverability. A reader still needs a continuous route onto the shown content and the ability to move over it to read it under magnification. Testing one point just inside the near edge after a mandatory rest establishes less than that. Rules 3 and 3b also give opposing answers for a no-rest sweep through a scatter tooltip over another point. Specify when the hoverable presentation begins, provide persistence over its readable extent, and give activation unconditionally to the real pick. \n- **2.5.7 and 2.5.8 AA.** Following a corridor with no button down is hover movement. The relevant dragging operation is selecting a zoom range. Buttons provide ordinary zoom and pan, but cannot reproduce an arbitrary two-endpoint selection. Two clicks or editable endpoints would provide that function. Rule 4’s 24×24 minimum is appropriate for displayed row buttons. Measuring the CSS box alone does not establish that an unobscured target is available to operate.  · \n- **2.4.11 AA.** It concerns a focused component being entirely hidden by author content. A partial overlap of the datum ring alone is insufficient to establish that failure. A2 expressly protects the indicator, and rule 6 needs an explicit exclusion for focused controls and the actual focus strokes. The fallback in #4 can entirely cover a control. \n- **2.4.13 is AAA.** It measures qualifying changed pixels between focused and unfocused states. A 2 px token with good adjacent contrast does not establish it. For example, on a plain 300×200 empty-window proxy with only the inset plot outline, the ideal ink area between insets 1 and 3 is 1,968 px². The rectangular reference perimeter requires 2,000 px². A thicker inset outline and focused/unfocused pixel comparison would support an AAA claim. This is separate from the AA release verdict. \n\n## Ten mutations the tests could miss\n\nThese are **reasoned from the committed assertions**. I did not run mutation tests. The strongest existing narrow-scatter regression does exercise synthetic `input`; the new area oracle does compare three plotted series. The remaining gaps are more specific than the earlier weak-test list.\n\n| Priority | Mutation that could survive | Assertion that would catch it |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Use the held datum for mouse activation only after a rested corridor crosses into another band. | Rest at Q3, cross into Q4, assert Q3 is still shown, then click and require **Q4’s exact ref and caller row**. The present tests split holding and click identity into separate sequences.  |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
